 "article": {
  "title": "The Village That Runs on Sunlight",
  "topic": "能源与乡村（光伏与储能）",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 187,
  "paras": [
   "Ten years ago this village of four hundred people had electricity for six hours a day. Now the rooftops are covered with panels, the school has air conditioning, and the clinic keeps vaccines cold through the night.",
   "The change did not come from a single large project. It came from many small ones: households bought panels as prices fell, a cooperative paid for a shared battery, and a young engineer came back from the city to run the maintenance. When a storm tore down the lines last winter, the village did not go dark.",
   "Storage, not sunshine, is the hard part. Sunlight is free at noon and absent at night, so a village needs batteries, and batteries need somebody who can check them every month. The cooperative now trains two residents a year, which is far cheaper than flying in a technician.",
   "What happened here is not a fairy tale about technology. It is a story about ownership. When people can read the meter, fix the panel and count the savings, they treat the system as theirs, and systems that are treated as theirs tend to last."
  ],
  "zh": "这个四百人的村庄十年前每天只有六小时电，如今屋顶铺满光伏板，学校装了空调，诊所整夜能冷藏疫苗。变化不靠一个大工程，而靠许多小项目：电价下降后各家自己买板，合作社出钱买下共用电池，一位年轻工程师从城里回来负责维护。去年冬天风暴刮断线路时，村子没有断电。真正的难点不是阳光而是储能：正午阳光免费、夜里没有，所以需要电池，而电池需要有人每月检查；合作社每年培训两名村民，比请技术员飞来便宜得多。这不是技术童话，而是关于所有权的故事——当人们能看表、能修板、能算清省了多少钱，他们才会把系统当成自己的，而当成自己的东西往往能活得久。",
  "hard": [
